.\" Public Domain.  After ts.4 and mtio.4, for SCSI tapes on a PC.
.\"
.\"	@(#)st.4	1.1 (2.11BSD) 2026/11/27
.\"
.TH ST 4 "November 27, 2026"
.UC 2
.SH NAME
st \- SCSI tapes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.ft B
.nf
/sys/conf/PC:
	NST	\fIst_drives\fP		# SCSI tapes, on the Adaptec

major device number(s):
	raw: 13
minor device encoding:
	bits 0003 specify the drive
	bit  0004 specifies no-rewind operation
	bit  0010 ignored
.fi
.ft R
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I st
device provides access to SCSI tape drives on an Adaptec AHA-1540 or
1542, the adapter the SCSI disks are on.
The bus is looked over when a tape is first opened, and each tape drive
found there becomes the next drive, in order of SCSI id, up to
.BR NST .
The kernel says what it found:
.PP
.nf
	st0: ARCHIVE VIPER 150 21247 2.10, id 2, async
.fi
.PP
Any SCSI tape drive is used the same way.
A drive whose least record is a byte, such as a DAT drive, is set to
records of any length: each write is a record of its own, and each read
takes one record, which must be no longer than the read.
Any other, such as a QIC drive, is read and written in blocks of the
size it is set to, 512 bytes for QIC: a read or a write must be of whole
blocks, and is of as many as it asks for, so that a write of 10240
bytes is twenty blocks.
A drive writes at the density it chooses for the tape, and reads
whatever density it finds.
.PP
The files are
.I rmt0
to
.IR rmt3 ,
which are rewound when they are closed, and
.I nrmt0
to
.I nrmt3
(the same as
.I rmt4
to
.IR rmt7 ),
which are not.
.I rmt8
to
.I rmt15
and
.I nrmt8
to
.I nrmt11
are the same again: upstream's drives took them for a second density,
and the programs open
.I /dev/rmt8
and
.I /dev/nrmt8
when they are given no tape.
There is no block device.
.PP
A tape opened to write and written, or opened only to write, has a
tape mark written at the end when it is closed: one, which ends the
file.
A read past the last file finds the end of what is written on the tape
and returns 0, as at a tape mark, so a tape ends with two ends of file,
as upstream's do with two marks.
A tape that is not rewound is left just after the mark, where the next
file may be written.
A read at a tape mark returns 0, and the next read is of the next file.
.PP
The
.I ioctl
calls of
.I <sys/mtio.h>
work on the raw files:
MTIOCTOP with MTWEOF (write tape marks), MTFSF and MTBSF (move forward
or back over tape marks), MTFSR and MTBSR (over records, or blocks), MTREW
(rewind), MTOFFL (rewind and unload), MTNOP, and MTFLUSH (write what the
drive holds, with no mark); MTIOCGET, whose
.I mt_type
is MT_ISST, with the bits MTF_EOM, MTF_WRTLCK and MTF_WRITTEN in the
high byte of
.IR mt_dsreg ,
the third byte of the last sense data the drive sent in
.I mt_erreg
with the additional sense code above it, and what the drive did not do of
the last command in
.IR mt_resid ;
and MTIOCIEOT and MTIOCEEOT.
Near the end of the tape a drive warns that it is there; after that
writes are refused with ENOSPC, unless MTIOCIEOT has said to let them
be, and there is room for the tape mark.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'/dev/nrmt?  'u
/dev/rmt?	rewound when closed
.br
/dev/nrmt?	not rewound
.DT
.SH "SEE ALSO"
mt(1),
tar(1),
tcopy(1),
dump(8),
restor(8)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
These are written on the terminal of whoever opened the tape, and the
last three in the system's log as well.
.PP
\fBst%d: no tape\fR.  The drive has no tape in it.
.PP
\fBst%d: not ready, sense key %d, code %x\fR.  The drive was not ready,
for the reason the codes give.
A drive that is loading a tape is waited for, for a minute.
.PP
\fBst%d: write protected\fR.  The tape was opened to write, and is
protected against writing.
.PP
\fBst%d: blocks of %D bytes unsupported\fR.  The drive's records are all
of a size larger than 32K.
.PP
\fBst%d: a record of %D bytes, read with %u\fR.  A read was shorter than
the record it found; the rest of the record is lost.
.PP
\fBst%d: a block not of %u bytes\fR.  The tape has blocks of another size
than the drive's.
.PP
\fBst%d: read: sense key %d, code %x\fR, and the same for a write.  The
drive failed to read or write, for the reason the codes give.
.SH BUGS
Only one adapter, and only unit 0 of a target.
A drive switched on after the bus was looked over is not found.
.PP
.I Lseek
does not move the tape, as upstream's raw tapes do not.
